  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-variatus-puente-escalanar/",
      "html": "<p>A wild strain of the variatus platy, available as German offspring. The ancestors were collected in 2002 in Puente Escalanar, Veracruz, Mexico by private hobbyists. To the supplier's knowledge, they have not yet appeared in regular trade.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-variatus-puente-escalanar/",
      "html": "<p>The ancestors of these animals were collected in 2002 in Puente Escalanar, Veracruz, Mexico.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-variatus-puente-escalanar/",
      "html": "<p>They are lively, very healthy animals, whose behaviour has remained very original. When they are worried, they join together to form a swarm, which is very rarely observed among the domesticated strains that have been around for a hundred years.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-variatus-puente-escalanar/",
      "html": "<p>A special characteristic is the sickle-shaped tail-root spot, which all specimens, males and females, show. Males have two basic coloration types: specimens with tiger stripes on the flanks of the front body, and specimens that are dotted there. Dominant males of the tigered variety become quince-yellow, while the dotted ones show much blue shimmer in the front body. Particularly beautiful are the sky-blue scales that some males of the tigered form show along the middle of the front body.</p>"
    }
  ],
